{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[A]A dandelion [D]born to [A]ramble
[A]Blowin' west [D]from the [A]Panhandle
[D]A daughter of the dust [A]bowl
[B]With her heart out in the [E]wind
[A]But a red dirt [D]girl's got a one-way [A]track
[A]From daddy's [D]fields to the two-room [A]shack
[D]The dust clouds turned her [A]days to black
[E]And her nights to [A]wonderin’
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[A]Where a girl as wild as a [D]western [A]town
[F#m]Can saddle up, not settle [E]down
[A]Grow as strong as a pinewood [D]tree
Turn that [A]red dirt to [E][A]Evergreen
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
A dandelion born to ramble
Said to goodbye to the panhandle
Now she’s tall in the saddle
With her heart up in the wind
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
So here’s to all the red dirt gals
Who dreams of greener fields and how
They’ll never let them hold us down
Or try to fence us in
{end_of_verse}
